// @emit-error emitProgram: a struct field typed as the union ARM `Circle` has no field rep — give the field the UNION's own type, or declare the arm as a plain struct
// A STRUCT FIELD TYPED AS A UNION ARM HAS NO FIELD CODE — D734, filed OPEN, and this file
// exists to keep the refusal LOUD and to keep it saying what it actually refuses.
//
// IT FIRES ON THE DECLARATIONS ALONE. There is no value anywhere in this program: the whole
// body is a `type` block. `collectS` codes every declared struct's fields before anything is
// emitted, and `nameIsStructDecl` excludes a variant by name ("their fields ride the variant
// tables, not the struct table"), so the code-15 rung declines and nothing else claims it.
//
// THE ABLATION IS FOUR ONE-LINE EDITS AND ALL FOUR RUN: delete the union (`Circle` becomes an
// ordinary struct, code 15); give the field the UNION (`g: Shape`, code 16, the box); make it
// an arm-element ARRAY (`g: Circle[]`, code 5, which resolves the arm through
// `variantIndexOf`); or declare a plain-struct layout twin. The `arm | null` niche
// (`g: Circle | null`) refuses identically.
//
// THE SENTENCE USED TO BE AN ALLOW-LIST — `only i32 / boolean / string / array struct fields
// are supported` — and that is FALSE of the compiler it belongs to: an f64 field is code 17,
// i64 23, f32 24, a MAP field 19, a nested STRUCT field 15, a union-BOX field 16, and the five
// scalar-list backings 25/26/27/6/4. Same shape of lie D702 removed from "nested arrays are
// not supported", and it costs the same thing: a reader who believes it looks for the wrong
// gap. Narrowing the message is not a substitute for building the lowering; the capability is
// still open and D734 says what it would take.
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type GW = { g: Circle }

function rd() { }
rd()
